#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Other Types of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c228c54-cb22-56c9-89e7-f009205ed1ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104256} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb20ecb-3cf6-58b5-bd9d-6b965a3762f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104257} {:answer/id #uuid "c4deb98e-cd6b-547a-acfc-ec16190f06c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104258} {:answer/id #uuid "d48460d1-1d9f-59d8-ad06-074f62eacc33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104259} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d0807b-7929-566e-938d-f243ec8608a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c228c54-cb22-56c9-89e7-f009205ed1ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104256} :answer-field/id #uuid "840c6519-ee5d-5b70-b43a-17d5816bc817" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104255}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "273e570a-a467-5f2a-8bbc-0b66a3856ac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-49022" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x}$ have a local minimum?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{x}+2x{e}^{x}=(x+2)x{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)x{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (x+2)x & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-2$ and $x=0$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =(-3+2)(-3){e}^{-3}=3{e}^{-3}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-1) & =(-1+2)(-1){e}^{-1}=-{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =(1+2)(1){e}^{1}=3e>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=0.$"}]}
